I found the built local CLI and the fixture has no separate token module—only a Tailwind theme configuration. I’m now running `context` from the fixture root so paths and project configuration are interpreted exactly as they would be for that app.

`crimes context src/Card.tsx` confirms medium overall risk: one medium accessibility finding and two low frontend findings. I’d refactor the card toward semantic markup and Tailwind’s existing token scale:

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  actionLabel: string;
  onActivate: () => void;
};

export function Card({
  children,
  actionLabel,
  onActivate,
}: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="w-full max-w-4xl border border-ink/20 bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-md">
      <div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
        <div className="text-lg md:col-span-2">{children}</div>

        <aside className="text-sm text-ink/70">sidebar</aside>
      </div>

      <button
        type="button"
        className="mt-6 rounded-md bg-brand px-4 py-2 text-surface focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-brand"
        onClick={onActivate}
      >
        {actionLabel}
      </button>
    </article>
  );
}
```

How this addresses each finding:

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: removes `onClick` from the non-semantic `<div>` and provides a real, visibly labelled `<button>`. This supplies keyboard activation, focusability, and button semantics natively. If activation navigates somewhere, use `<a href>` instead. Merely adding `role` and `tabIndex` would still require correctly implementing Enter/Space handlers.
- `design_token_escape`: replaces inline hex, RGB, shadow, padding, and font-size literals with the configured `brand`, `surface`, and `ink` colors plus Tailwind spacing, typography, radius, and shadow tokens.
- `responsive_fragility`: replaces the fixed `800`, `720`, and `240` widths and fixed height with `w-full`, a tokenized maximum width, and a grid that changes from one column to three at the configured `md` breakpoint. Typography uses the tokenized `text-lg`/`text-sm` scale.

The context report found no likely tests and reports a top-quartile test gap. I’d add a component test proving that the labelled button is keyboard-reachable and invokes `onActivate`; responsive layout can be covered with a small viewport-level visual or browser test. No files were changed.
